\\|p_{n}\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}\\,,\\qquad p_{n}\\in{\\cal P}_{n}\\,,$$  |\nL39: \nL40: namely the constant\nL41: \nL42:  | $$c_{n}(\\lambda):=\\sup_{p_{n}\\in{\\cal P}_{n}}\\frac{\\|p_{n}^{\\prime}\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}}{\\|p_{n}\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}}\\,.$$  |\nL43: \nL44: We derive explicit lower and upper bounds for the Markov constant $c_{n}(\\lambda)$, which are valid for all $n$ and $\\lambda$.\nL45: \nL46: MSC 2010: 41A17\nL47: \nL48: Key words and phrases: Markov type inequalities, Gegenbauer polynomials, matrix norms\nL49: ## 1 Introduction\nL50: \nL51: Let $w_{\\lambda}(t):=(1-t^{2})^{\\lambda-1/2}$, where $\\lambda>-\\frac{1}{2}$, be the Gegenbauer weight function, let $\\|\\cdot\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}$ be the associated $L_{2}$-norm,\nL52: \nL53:  | $$\\|f\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}=\\left\\{\\int_{-1}^{1}|f(x)|^{2}w_{\\lambda}(x)\\,dx\\right\\}^{1/2}\\,,$$  |\nL54: \nL55: and denote by ${\\cal P}_{n}$ the space of algebraic polynomials of degree $\\leq n$. In this paper, we study the best constant $c_{n}(\\lambda)$ in the Markov inequality in this norm\nL56:  | $$\\|p_{n}^{\\prime}\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}\\leq c_{n}(\\lambda)\\|p_{n}\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}\\,,\\qquad p_{n}\\in{\\cal P}_{n}\\,,$$  |  | (1.1)\nL57: \nL58: namely the constant\nL59: \nL60:  | $$c_{n}(\\lambda):=\\sup_{p_{n}\\in{\\cal P}_{n}}\\frac{\\|p_{n}^{\\prime}\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}}{\\|p_{n}\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}}\\,.$$  |\nL61: \nL62: Our goal is to derive good and explicit lower and upper bounds for the Markov constant $c_{n}(\\lambda)$ which are valid for all $n$ and $\\lambda$, i.e., to find constants $\\underline{c}(n,\\lambda)$ and $\\overline{c}(n,\\lambda)$ such that\nL63:  | $$\\underline{c}(n,\\lambda)\\leq c_{n}(\\lambda)\\leq\\overline{c}(n,\\lambda)\\,,$$  |\nL64: \nL65: with a small ratio $\\frac{\\overline{c}(n,\\lambda)}{\\underline{c}(n,\\lambda)}$.\nL66: \nL67: It is known that, for a fixed $\\lambda$, $c_{n}(\\lambda)$ grows like ${\\cal O}(n^{2})$, and that the asymptotic value\nL68: \nL69:  | $$c_{*}(\\lambda):=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{c_{n}(\\lambda)}{n^{2}}$$  |\nL70: is equal to $1/(2\\,j_{\\frac{2\\lambda-3}{4}})$, with $j_{\\nu}$ being the first positive zero of the Bessel function $J_{\\nu}$, see [cite15†2 , Thms. 1.1–1.3], whereby it can be shown that $c_{*}(\\lambda)$ behaves like ${\\cal O}(\\lambda^{-1})$. There is also a number of more precise results.\nL71: \nL72: For $\\lambda=\\frac{1}{2}$ (the constant weight $w_{\\frac{1}{2}}\\equiv 1$), it follows from the Schmidt result [cite16†4 ] that\nL73:  | $$\\frac{1}{\\pi}(n+\\mbox{\\large${\\textstyle\\frac{3}{2}}$})^{2}\\leq c_{n}(\\mbox{\\large${\\textstyle\\frac{1}{2}}$})\\leq\\frac{1}{\\pi}(n+2)^{2}\\,.$$  |\nL74: \nL75: For $\\lambda=0,1$ (the Chebyshev weights $w_{0}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}$ and $w_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}$, respectively), Nikolov [cite17†3 ] proved that\nL76: \nL77:  | $$\\begin{array}[]{l}0.472135n^{2}\\leq c_{n}(0)\\leq 0.478849(n+2)^{2}\\,,\\\\[2.15277pt]\nL78: 0.248549n^{2}\\leq c_{n}(1)\\leq 0.256861(n+\\mbox{\\large${\\textstyle\\frac{5}{2}}$})^{2}\\,.\\end{array}$$  |  | (1.2)\nL79: In [cite18†1 ], we obtained an upper bound valid for all $n$ and $\\lambda$,\nL80: \nL81:  | $$c_{n}(\\lambda)\\leq\\frac{(n+1)(n+2\\lambda+1)}{2\\sqrt{2\\lambda+1}}\\,,$$  |  | (1.3)\nL82: \nL83: however, the already mentioned asymptotics $c_{*}(\\lambda)={\\cal O}(\\lambda^{-1})$ shows that this result is not optimal.\nL84: \nL85: The main result of this paper is lower and upper bounds for $c_{n}(\\lambda)$ which are uniform with respect to $n$ and $\\lambda$. They show, in particular, that\nL86:  | $$[c_{n}(\\lambda)]^{2}\\asymp\\frac{1}{\\lambda^{2}}n(n+2\\lambda)^{3}\\,.$$  |\nL87: \nL88: For $n=1,2$ the exact values of the Markov constant are easily computable:\nL89: \nL90:  | $$[c_{1}(\\lambda)]^{2}=2(1+\\lambda),\\qquad[c_{2}(\\lambda)]^{2}=\\frac{4(2+\\lambda)(2+2\\lambda)}{2\\lambda+1}\\,.$$  |  | (1.4)\nL91: \nL92: Therefore, we consider below the case $n\\geq 3$. Our main result is\nL93: ###### Theorem 1.1\nL94: \nL95: For all $\\lambda>-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $n\\geq 3$, the best constant $c_{n}(\\lambda)$ in the Markov inequality\nL96: \nL97:  | $$\\|p_{n}^{\\prime}\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}\\leq c_{n}(\\lambda)\\|p_{n}\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}\\,,\\qquad p_{n}\\in{\\cal P}_{n}\\,,$$  |\nL98: \nL99: admits the estimates\nL100:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{n^{2}(n+\\lambda)^{2}}{(\\lambda+1)(\\lambda+2)}\\;<$  | $\\displaystyle[c_{n}(\\lambda)]^{2}$  | $\\displaystyle<\\;\\frac{n(n+2\\lambda+2)^{3}}{(\\lambda+2)(\\lambda+3)},\\qquad\\quad\\lambda\\geq 2\\,;$  |  | (1.5)\nL101:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{(n+\\lambda)^{2}(n+2\\lambda^{\\prime})^{2}}{(2\\lambda+1)(2\\lambda+5)}\\;<$  | $\\displaystyle[c_{n}(\\lambda)]^{2}$  | $\\displaystyle<\\;\\frac{(n+\\lambda+\\lambda^{\\prime\\prime}+2)^{4}}{2(2\\lambda+1)\\sqrt{2\\lambda+5}},\\qquad\\lambda>-\\mbox{\\large${\\textstyle\\frac{1}{2}}$}\\,,$  |  | (1.6)\nL102: where $\\lambda^{\\prime}=\\min\\,\\{0,\\lambda\\}$, $\\,\\lambda^{\\prime\\prime}=\\max\\,\\{0,\\lambda\\}$.\nL103: \nL104: As a consequence, we can specify the following bounds for the asymptotic value $c_{*}(\\lambda)$:\nL105: ###### Corollary 1.2\nL106: \nL107: For any $\\lambda>-\\frac{1}{2}$, the asymptotic Markov constant $c_{*}(\\lambda)=\\lim\\limits_{n\\to\\infty}n^{-2}c_{n}(\\lambda)$ satisfies the inequalities\nL108: \nL109:  | $$\\frac{1}{(2\\lambda+1)(2\\lambda+5)}<[c_{*}(\\lambda)]^{2}<\\begin{cases}\\,\\displaystyle{\\frac{1}{2(2\\lambda+1)\\sqrt{2\\lambda+5}}}\\,,&\\quad-\\frac{1}{2}<\\lambda\\leq\\lambda^{*}\\,,\\\\\nL110: \\,\\displaystyle{\\frac{1}{(\\lambda+2)(\\lambda+3)}}\\,,&\\quad\\lambda>\\lambda^{*}\\,,\\end{cases}$$  |\nL111: \nL112: where $\\lambda^{*}\\approx 25$ .\nL113: The lower bound in ()e1] follows from that in ()e2] and is less accurate, we put it in this form to make the comparison between the two bounds in ()e1] more obvious.\nL114: \nL115: The upper bound in ()e2] does not have the right order ${\\cal O}(n^{4}/\\lambda^{2})$ in $\\,\\lambda\\,$ (for $\\lambda$ fixed), however this bound serves not only for the case $-\\frac{1}{2}<\\lambda<2$, but for a fixed $\\lambda\\in[2,\\lambda^{*}]$ and $n\\geq n_{0}(\\lambda)$ it is also better than the one in ()e1].\nL116: In the next corollary, we set $\\lambda=0,1$ in the upper estimate ()e2], and that improves the upper estimates in ()n] for the Chebyshev weights. When coupled with the lower estimate from ()n], this gives rather tight bounds.\nL117: ###### Corollary 1.3\nL118: \nL119: For the Chebyshev weights $w_{0}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}$ and $w_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}$, we have\nL120: \nL121:  | $\\displaystyle 0.472135\\,n^{2}\\leq c_{n}(0)\\leq 0.472871\\,(n+2)^{2}\\,,$  |\nL122:  | $\\displaystyle 0.248549\\,n^{2}\\leq c_{n}(1)\\leq 0.250987\\,(n+4)^{2}\\,.$  |\nL123: \nL124: The lower and upper estimates in ()e1] have different orders with respect to $\\lambda$. However we can get a perfect match with slightly less accurate constants.\nL125: ###### Theorem 1.4\nL126: \nL127: For all $\\lambda\\geq 7$ and $n\\geq 3$, the best constant $c_{n}(\\lambda)$ in the Markov inequality satisfies\nL128: \nL129:  | $$\\frac{1}{16}\\frac{n(n+2\\lambda)^{3}}{\\lambda^{2}}\\leq[c_{n}(\\lambda)]^{2}\\leq\\frac{n(n+2\\lambda)^{3}}{\\lambda^{2}}\\,.$$  |  | (1.7)\nL130: ###### Corollary 1.5\nL131: \nL132: For the Markov constant $c_{n}(\\lambda)$ we have the following asymptotic estimates:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the Markov inequality in the 𝐿_2-norm with the Gegenbauer weight (https://arxiv.org/html/1701.07682v1)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view0\",\"pattern\":\"uniform\"}); Total lines: 858\nL31: ###### Abstract\nL32: \nL33: Let $w_{\\lambda}(t):=(1-t^{2})^{\\lambda-1/2}$, where $\\lambda>-\\frac{1}{2}$, be the Gegenbauer weight function, let $\\|\\cdot\\|_{w_{\\lambda}}$ be the as