tyle{\\frac{1}{(\\lambda+2)(\\lambda+3)}}\\,,&\\quad\\lambda>\\lambda^{*}\\,,\\end{cases}$$  |\nL111: \nL112: where $\\lambda^{*}\\approx 25$ .\nL113: The lower bound in ()e1] follows from that in ()e2] and is less accurate, we put it in this form to make the comparison between the two bounds in ()e1] more obvious.\nL114: \nL115: The upper bound in ()e2] does not have the right order ${\\cal O}(n^{4}/\\lambda^{2})$ in $\\,\\lambda\\,$ (for $\\lambda$ fixed), however this bound serves not only for the case $-\\frac{1}{2}<\\lambda<2$, but for a fixed $\\lambda\\in[2,\\lambda^{*}]$ and $n\\geq n_{0}(\\lambda)$ it is also better than the one in ()e1].\nL116: In the next corollary, we set $\\lambda=0,1$ in the upper estimate ()e2], and that improves the upper estimates in ()n] for the Chebyshev weights. When coupled with the lower estimate from ()n], this gives rather tight bounds.\nL117: ###### Corollary 1.3\nL118: \nL119: For the Chebyshev weights $w_{0}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}$ and $w_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}$, we have\nL120: \nL121:  | $\\displaystyle 0.472135\\,n^{2}\\leq c_{n}(0)\\leq 0.472871\\,(n+2)^{2}\\,,$  |\nL122:  | $\\displaystyle 0.248549\\,n^{2}\\leq c_{n}(1)\\leq 0.250987\\,(n+4)^{2}\\,.$  |\nL123: \nL124: The lower and upper estimates in ()e1] have different orders with respect to $\\lambda$. However we can get a perfect match with slightly less accurate constants.\nL125: ###### Theorem 1.4\nL126: \nL127: For all $\\lambda\\geq 7$ and $n\\geq 3$, the best constant $c_{n}(\\lambda)$ in the Markov inequality satisfies\nL128: \nL129:  | $$\\frac{1}{16}\\frac{n(n+2\\lambda)^{3}}{\\lambda^{2}}\\leq[c_{n}(\\lambda)]^{2}\\leq\\frac{n(n+2\\lambda)^{3}}{\\lambda^{2}}\\,.$$  |  | (1.7)\nL130: ###### Corollary 1.5\nL131: \nL132: For the Markov constant $c_{n}(\\lambda)$ we have the following asymptotic estimates:\nL133: \nL134: i)   $\\displaystyle{\\sqrt{n}\\;\\leq\\lim_{\\lambda\\to\\infty}\\frac{c_{n}(\\lambda)}{\\sqrt{2\\lambda}}\\leq\\sqrt{3n}}\\,$;\nL135: \nL136: ii)   $\\displaystyle(n-\\mbox{\\large${\\textstyle\\frac{1}{2}}$})(n-1)\\leq\\lim_{\\lambda\\to-\\frac{1}{2}}c_{n}(\\lambda)\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2\\lambda+1}\\leq(n+\\mbox{\\large${\\textstyle\\frac{3}{2}}$})^{2}$ .\nL137: Part ii) follows from ()e2]. Though part i) does not formally follow from Theorem cite19†1.4 , it follows from a part of its proof.\nL138: \nL139: Let us describe briefly how these results are obtained.\nL140: \nL141: It is well-known that the squared best constant in the Markov inequality in the $L_{2}$-norm with arbitrary (and possibly different) weights for $p$ and $p^{\\prime}$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of a certain positive definite matrix, in our case we have\nL142:  | $$[c_{n}(\\lambda)]^{2}=\\mu_{\\max}({\\mathbf{B}}_{n})\\,,$$  |  | (1.8)\nL143: \nL144: where the matrix ${\\mathbf{B}}_{n}$ is specified in Sect. 2. We obtain then lower and upper bounds for $\\mu_{\\max}({\\mathbf{B}}_{n})$ using three values associated with the matrix ${\\mathbf{B}}_{n}$ and its eigenvalues $(\\mu_{i})$ (note that $\\mu_{i}>0$):\nL145: \nL146: a) the trace\nL147: \nL148:  | $${\\rm tr\\,}({\\mathbf{B}}_{n}):=\\sum b_{ii}=\\sum\\mu_{i}\\,;$$  |\nL149: \nL150: b) the max-norm\nL151: \nL152:  | $$\\|{\\mathbf{B}}_{n}\\|_{\\infty}=\\max_{i}\\sum_{j}|b_{ij}|\\,;$$  |\nL153: c) the Frobenius norm\nL154: \nL155:  | $$\\|{\\mathbf{B}}_{n}\\|_{F}^{2}:=\\sum_{i,j}|b_{ij}|^{2}={\\rm tr\\,}({\\mathbf{B}}_{n}{\\mathbf{B}}_{n}^{T})=\\sum\\mu_{i}^{2}\\,.$$  |\nL156: \nL157: Clearly, we have\nL158: \nL159:  | $${\\rm i)}\\quad\\mu_{\\max}\\leq{\\rm tr\\,}({\\mathbf{B}}_{n})\\,,\\qquad{\\rm ii)}\\quad\\mu_{\\max}\\leq\\|{\\mathbf{B}}_{n}\\|_{\\infty}\\,,\\qquad{\\rm iii)}\\quad\\mu_{\\max}\\leq\\|{\\mathbf{B}}_{n}\\|_{F}\\,,$$  |  | (1.9)\nL160: and generally $\\mu_{\\max}\\leq\\|{\\mathbf{B}}_{n}\\|_{*}$, where $\\|\\cdot\\|_{*}$ is any matrix norm. The upper estimate ()l_1] cited from [cite18†1 ] is exactly the first inequality $\\mu_{\\max}\\leq{\\rm tr\\,}({\\mathbf{B}}_{n})$, and as we noted, this estimate is not optimal. The better upper bounds ()e1]-()e2] in Theorem cite20†1.1 are obtained from (cite21†1.9 .ii) and (cite21†1.9 .iii), respectively.\nL161: \nL162: For the lower bounds we use the inequalities\nL163:  | $${\\rm i^{\\prime})}\\quad\\mu_{\\max}\\geq\\frac{\\sum\\mu_{i}^{2}}{\\sum\\mu_{i}}=\\frac{\\|{\\mathbf{B}}_{n}\\|_{F}^{2}}{{\\rm tr\\,}({\\mathbf{B}}_{n})}\\,,\\qquad{\\rm ii^{\\prime})}\\quad\\mu_{\\max}({\\mathbf{B}}_{n})\\geq\\max_{i}b_{ii}\\,.$$  |  | (1.10)\nL164: \nL165: Inequality (i’) gives the lower estimates in ()e1]-()e2], and combination of (i’) and (ii’) yields the lower bound in ()e4].\nL166: The paper is organised as follows. In Sect. cite7†2 , following our previous studies [cite18†1 ], we give an explicit form of the matrix ${\\mathbf{B}}_{n}$ appearing in ()B]. Sects. cite7†2 -cite9†4 contain some auxiliary inequalities. In Sect. cite10†5 , we find an upper bound for the max-norm $\\|{\\mathbf{B}}_{n}\\|_{\\infty}$, and in Sect .cite11†6 we give both lower and upper estimates for the Frobinuis norm $\\|{\\mathbf{B}}_{n}\\|_{F}$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the Markov inequality in the 𝐿_2-norm with the Gegenbauer weight (https://arxiv.org/html/1701.07682v1)\nciteturn5view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view0\",\"pattern\":\"classical Markov\"}); Total lines: 858\nNo matching text found for \"classical Markov\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the Markov inequality in the 𝐿_2-norm with the Gegenbauer weight (https://arxiv.org/html/1701.07682v1)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn4view0\",\"pattern\":\"sup\"}); Total lines: 858\nNo matching text found for \"sup\""

